  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/mastacembelus-albomaculatus",
      "html": "<p>Mastacembelus albomaculatus is a spiny eel endemic to Lake Tanganyika, where it inhabits rocky areas around the shoreline. Rarely seen in the hobby, it suits a Tanganyikan community of species too large to be considered food, and its hard, alkaline water requirements rule out most other freshwater tanks. The stiffened dorsal rays that give spiny eels their name mean care is needed when handling it.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/mastacembelus-albomaculatus",
      "html": "<p>Endemic to Lake Tanganyika (Burundi, DR Congo, Tanzania and Zambia).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/mastacembelus-albomaculatus",
      "html": "<p>It inhabits rocky areas around the shoreline of the lake.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "aquariumSize",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/mastacembelus-albomaculatus",
      "html": "<p>Standard length 22 cm SL. Aquarium base 120 × 30 cm. A tank of 120 × 30 × 30 cm holds about 108 litres.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "maintenance",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/mastacembelus-albomaculatus",
      "html": "<p>Use piles of rocks to form sheltered areas. It is essential that the tank bottom is covered in sand as the fish spends much of its time burrowed into the substrate. Gravel is not recommended, as its sharp edges can damage the eel's skin. There should be no strong currents in the tank.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/mastacembelus-albomaculatus",
      "html": "<ul><li>Temperature: 23–27°C</li><li>pH: 8.0–9.5</li><li>Hardness: 8–25 dGH</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/mastacembelus-albomaculatus",
      "html": "<p>In nature, it preys on small fish but aquarium specimens readily accept dead alternatives. Prawn, cockle, mussel, earthworms and bloodworm can all be offered.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/mastacembelus-albomaculatus",
      "html": "<p>It is peaceful with species it cannot fit into its mouth but is not suitable for most freshwater tanks, due to its hard, alkaline water requirements. It is best kept in a Tanganyikan community, where it can be combined with any species too large to be considered food. It tends to be somewhat territorial with conspecifics and a large tank is required if more than one is to be kept.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/mastacembelus-albomaculatus",
      "html": "<p>Unknown.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/mastacembelus-albomaculatus",
      "html": "<p>Not thought to have been bred in aquaria.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/mastacembelus-albomaculatus",
      "html": "<p>This is a great oddball species for the Tanganyikan community tank, although it is not as attractive as the closely related Aethiomastacembelus ellipsifer. It is rarely seen in the hobby. The species in the family Mastacembelidae are known as \"spiny\" eels because the dorsal rays are stiffened. Care must therefore be exercised when handling them to avoid injuries. Spiny eels also have no swim bladder, an adaptation to their benthic lifestyle.</p>"
    }
  ],
